WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
Model output should be treated as untrusted until it is validated for the destination.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:14.920
If the application expects structured JSON, use schema validation concepts, type checks, allowed values, and business-rule validation.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:22.940
If the output will be rendered in a user interface, apply safe rendering and output encoding appropriate to the context.

4
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:29.520
If the output will influence a decision, review the evidence and uncertainty before accepting it as fact.

5
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.120
Hallucination and uncertainty are operational security concerns.

6
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:42.300
A confident but incorrect summary, citation, recommendation, or classification can lead users to make bad decisions.

7
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:52.640
Secure integrations should ground answers in evidence when possible, include citations or source references where useful, and avoid presenting generated text as verified truth.

8
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.940
Human review or independent checks should be required when the impact is high.

9
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.140
Logging should support troubleshooting without becoming a data leak.

10
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:16.240
Prompts, responses, retrieval content, tool calls, provider errors, and user feedback can contain secrets, personal data, source code, customer information, or sensitive business context.

11
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:25.380
Redaction, minimization, retention limits, access controls, and safe event design help preserve observability while limiting exposure.

12
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:31.560
Logs should show outcomes and decisions without unnecessarily storing the full sensitive context.

13
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:38.160
Testing and monitoring keep integrations healthy as models, prompts, providers, tools, and workflows change.

14
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:49.940
Regression tests can cover prompt behavior, structured output, retrieval authorization, tool argument validation, denied actions, and known failure cases at a high level.

15
00:01:50.040 --> 00:02:04.440
Monitoring should watch unusual requests, denied actions, sensitive-data access, tool failures, cost spikes, latency, provider errors, repeated unsafe requests, and incident signals.

16
00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:11.980
Incident response should include leakage, unsafe actions, provider failures, and misconfiguration.
